From a4079a8d0311e774ac684d903ef1109b3ef10f60 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oleksii Zakharov <contact@zalexdev.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 02:11:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] um: x86: add the missing 32-bit stub_seccomp_save_state

The generic stub now calls stub_seccomp_save_state() on entry, symmetric
with the existing restore, so every subarch has to provide one. x86-64 got
an empty version; x86-32 was missed and would fail to build.

Empty for the same reason as x86-64: the TLS entries can only change
through set_thread_area(), a syscall the kernel already sees, so its idea
of them is never stale.

Not build-tested: no 32-bit libc headers are available in this environment,
and none can be fetched. Compile-tested by inspection only, which is worth
stating plainly rather than implying more.

Signed-off-by: Oleksii Zakharov <contact@zalexdev.com>
---
 arch/x86/um/shared/sysdep/stub_32.h | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/x86/um/shared/sysdep/stub_32.h b/arch/x86/um/shared/sysdep/stub_32.h
index b249198e4dfdc..c8f5014ea35c3 100644
--- a/arch/x86/um/shared/sysdep/stub_32.h
+++ b/arch/x86/um/shared/sysdep/stub_32.h
@@ -134,6 +134,17 @@ static __always_inline void *get_stub_data(void)
 		:: "i" (STUB_SIZE),					\
 		   "i" (&fn))
 
+/*
+ * Nothing to capture: the TLS entries can only be changed through
+ * set_thread_area(), which is a syscall the kernel already intercepts, so its
+ * idea of them is never stale. See the arm64 version, where the guest can
+ * change its thread pointer without any syscall at all.
+ */
+static __always_inline void
+stub_seccomp_save_state(struct stub_data_arch *arch)
+{
+}
+
 static __always_inline void
 stub_seccomp_restore_state(struct stub_data_arch *arch)
 {
